From: OpenWrt scratch debug
Subject: DEBUG: net: airoha: trace RX ring refill failures

DEBUG-only diagnostic patch, part of an investigation into a silent
DHCP-reply (and PPPoE PADO) receive loss on the Nokia XG-040G-MF
(Airoha AN7583).

airoha_qdma_fill_rx_queue() tops each RX ring back up to ndesc-1
posted descriptors after every NAPI poll. If page_pool_dev_alloc_frag()
fails, the loop breaks silently and the ring is left under-filled;
no counter, no log line. The only thing that can trigger another
fill attempt for that ring is a NEW packet arriving on it (which
schedules its NAPI poll) - so a ring that drains all the way to zero
posted descriptors before a fill attempt succeeds again can never
receive another frame: nothing will ever trigger its NAPI poll again,
so fill_rx_queue() never runs again for it either. That is a genuine
hardware deadlock, not a transient drop, and it would explain why the
DHCP/PPPoE loss never self-recovers once it starts.

This is particularly plausible for RX queue 4, which upstream (and
Airoha's own reference SDK) size at only 16 descriptors and route
essentially all "VIP" force-to-CPU control traffic through: PPPoE
Discovery, every PPP negotiation stage (LCP/IPCP/CHAP/PAP/IPv6CP),
ISAKMP, BOOTP, DHCPv6, SIP, FTP control, LLDP, and more, all sharing
the same tiny ring. A burst of several of these in quick succession
(e.g. the LCP/IPCP/CHAP/PAP cascade right after a PPPoE PADO) could
plausibly drain 16 descriptors faster than NAPI can refill them,
especially if page_pool allocation stalls even once during the burst.
This would also explain why PPPoE fails in ~1.5-2 minutes while DHCP
takes ~36-45 minutes: PPPoE drives far more traffic through this same
shared ring in a short window than DHCP's occasional renewal does.

None of the existing 4 RX-drop trace points (see
925-DEBUG-an7583-trace-rx-classify-drop.patch) cover this path - they
all instrument airoha_qdma_rx_process() (handling of an already-
arrived descriptor), not airoha_qdma_fill_rx_queue() (posting fresh
descriptors back to the ring). This patch adds a rate-limited
pr_warn() at the refill failure point, logging the ring index, how
many descriptors are currently posted vs. the ring size, and how many
were successfully refilled before the failure. If this fires
repeatedly for queue 4 in the runup to a DHCP/PPPoE failure, with
"queued" trending toward zero, that would directly confirm the
descriptor-exhaustion/deadlock mechanism.

Not for upstream submission.
---
--- a/drivers/net/ethernet/airoha/airoha_eth.c
+++ b/drivers/net/ethernet/airoha/airoha_eth.c
@@ -680,8 +680,12 @@
 
 		page = page_pool_dev_alloc_frag(q->page_pool, &offset,
 						q->buf_size);
-		if (!page)
+		if (!page) {
+			if (net_ratelimit())
+				pr_warn("airoha_eth: DEBUG rx refill fail qid=%d queued=%d ndesc=%d filled=%d\n",
+					qid, q->queued, q->ndesc, nframes);
 			break;
+		}
 
 		q->head = (q->head + 1) % q->ndesc;
 		q->queued++;
